---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/postscript';
const title = 'Postscript for Shopify: workflow trial, costs, and alternatives';
const desc = 'Evaluate Postscript with a product-release trial, current pricing sources, purchase-exit checks, and a comparison with your existing email platform.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <section class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/apps">All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Shopify messaging evaluation</p>
    <h1>Postscript for Shopify: test the workflow before adding another sender</h1>
    <p>Postscript is a candidate when your immediate problem is running Shopify messaging campaigns from store data. Its value to your team depends on audience accuracy, the work required to maintain flows, reply handling, and the bill at your send volume. This page provides a proposed evaluation, not a hands-on benchmark or a merchant success story.</p>
    <h2>What the current product sources establish</h2>
    <p>Checked November 25, 2026: the <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/postscript-sms-marketing">developer's Shopify listing</a> advertises personalized campaigns and automations using historical Shopify data, segmentation, and two-way messaging. It now describes SMS, RCS, and email, with email rolling out in phases. Confirm your account's access before planning to replace an existing email service.</p>
    <p>The listing names Klaviyo among supported integrations. That establishes an advertised integration, not proof that every suppression or custom property you use will synchronize in the way your campaigns need. Make the required fields, event timing, and system ownership explicit in the trial.</p>
    <h2>When to shortlist it, and when to compare first</h2>
    <p>Shortlist Postscript if you need to improve a specific SMS workflow and are prepared to manage that workflow separately from other channels. A limited release is a useful evaluation because audience membership, variant availability, timing, and purchases all affect whether a message should go out.</p>
    <p>If you already operate email and SMS successfully in Klaviyo or Omnisend, first identify the missing capability or recurring manual work. Moving a working send to another platform adds event mapping and scheduling work. Ask each candidate to demonstrate the same audience and purchase-exit scenario; do not compare one vendor's polished demo with another vendor's untouched account.</p>
    <p>Attentive is another candidate for the same buying exercise: its <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/attentive">listing</a> describes email and SMS, acquisition units, and strategy support. Ask what service is included in the actual proposal and who will own execution. Store revenue alone does not determine the right platform. See <a href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a> for the adjacent decision.</p>
    <h2>Trial brief: access to a limited product release</h2>
    <p>This is a proposed exercise with test data. Define a product or variant, release time, relevant interest signal, and recent-buyer exclusion. Specify which system stores the interest signal. Purchase history alone may not identify the right size, style, or flavor; do not infer a preference you have never collected.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Create test contacts covering an eligible subscriber, an opted-out contact, a recent purchaser, and a contact missing the preference field. Inspect membership before scheduling.</li>
      <li>Change a preference and place a test purchase. Record when the changes reach the messaging system and whether they remove the contact from the intended send.</li>
      <li>Preview the exact variant URL on a phone. Check inventory, shipping expectations, any code, and the fallback when the variant sells out.</li>
      <li>Test cancellation of a queued message. Record who can pause the campaign and who handles a reply asking for help.</li>
      <li>Compare the SMS schedule with email. Make one person responsible for removing duplicate or conflicting offers.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Acceptance means the intended audience is reproducible, exclusions work at send time as required, and the team can explain failures. If an event arrives late, document the observed delay and decide whether a longer buffer or a different workflow is needed. These checks do not establish a conversion improvement.</p>
    <h2>Cart recovery needs a separate purchase-exit test</h2>
    <p>Use a test checkout, then purchase before the reminder is due. Check whether the reminder is cancelled, what happens when the purchase event arrives late, and whether an email flow is already contacting the same shopper. Assign a recovery owner across systems instead of relying on two independent schedules.</p>
    <p>Start the message with a useful route back to checkout rather than an automatic discount. A code may be appropriate for a defined audience, but a shopper who would have purchased at full price creates no incremental benefit from the incentive alone. Verify that the landing page retains the intended cart and that unavailable products do not make the message misleading.</p>
    <h2>Price the actual message mix</h2>
    <p>The <a href="https://postscript.io/pricing">official pricing page</a>, checked November 25, lists Starter at $0 monthly subscription with a $49 monthly minimum spend, and $0.009 per SMS or $0.045 per MMS plus carrier fees. Growth lists $100 monthly and $0.008 per SMS or $0.03 per MMS plus carrier fees. These displayed rates are a starting point; confirm destination, billable units, included usage, minimum treatment, and contract terms for your account.</p>
    <p>For illustration, 10,000 billable SMS units at those displayed rates produce $90 in Starter usage or $80 in Growth usage before carrier fees and other charges. Adding the displayed Growth subscription produces $180 before those charges. This deliberately excludes minimum-spend interpretation and extras; have the vendor reconcile the full quote. A lower unit rate does not automatically make the higher plan cheaper at your volume.</p>
    <p>Model a peak release month with separate rows for welcome, recovery, broadcasts, and MMS. Preview billable segments for the actual copy rather than assuming one recipient equals one unit. Include migration labor and any paid services in the comparison. Use <a href="/guides/pricing">the pricing guide</a> to organize the worksheet.</p>
    <h2>Measure the release without inventing lift</h2>
    <p>Record eligible recipients, messages billed, delivered messages where available, purchases, net sales, discounts, returns, and reply workload. Use the same denominator when comparing sends. A smaller audience can produce a higher revenue-per-recipient figure even while producing less total contribution.</p>
    <p>For a live pilot with sufficient volume, randomly reserve an eligible holdout and compare purchase outcomes over the same window. Account for inventory depletion: an unsent group cannot buy a sold-out product later, so release timing can complicate the interpretation. Report the limitation rather than claiming all attributed revenue was caused by SMS.</p>
    <h2>Migration checklist: permissions before campaigns</h2>
    <p>Preserve opt-in evidence and opt-outs before moving active contacts. Identify the originating system for each field and reconcile imported counts against the export. Email subscription status alone is not SMS permission. Do not restart a historical recovery flow for everyone imported into a new account.</p>
    <p>Pause duplicate automations during the handover. Check one new signup, one unsubscribe, one purchase exit, and one failed or delayed event in the new arrangement. Retain a rollback plan that prevents both providers from sending the same message. Confirm current destination and sending restrictions with the provider for the audience you plan to contact.</p>
    <h2>Keep the calendar small enough to own</h2>
    <p>Write down which messages count toward your promotional frequency policy and how transactional notices are handled. A launch, replenishment reminder, and cart flow may all reach the same person. Review combined exposure and opt-out patterns instead of setting an unsupported universal monthly cap.</p>
    <p>Keep longer education in the channel where the customer can use it. If evaluating Sequenzy or any separate email tool, verify data exchange and suppression explicitly before assuming it will coordinate with Postscript. There is no demonstrated integration or cost advantage established for that pairing on this page.</p>
    <h2>Decision record to take away</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>First workflow and the store fields it requires.</li>
      <li>Observed audience-update delay and purchase-exit behavior.</li>
      <li>Peak-month quote with all fees and message units explained.</li>
      <li>Owner for replies, pauses, and cross-channel scheduling.</li>
      <li>Reason to choose Postscript over improving the current sender.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Continue with <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>, <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email and SMS coordination</a>, or <a href="/use-cases/product-launch">product-launch planning</a> according to the unresolved workflow. A back-in-stock program needs a separate availability and waitlist assessment; see <a href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">back-in-stock planning</a>.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
